{"t":1791494345,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"0c148a29-72d8-47d5-8987-03ba60bd3971","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-08T18:36:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 081836\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n236 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\n\nIncreasing confidence in tidal flooding later this weekend due \nto increased southerly flow combined with high astronomical \ntides. See the Coastal Flooding section below for more \ninformation. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Sunny & mild conditions continue through the end of the\n  week. \n\n- 2) Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to arrive Saturday night.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Sunny & mild conditions continue through the end\nof the week. \n\nCurrent surface analysis depicts that the cold front has largely \npushed through the region this morning. In its wake, dry air builds \nin from the west as high pressure moves in overhead. A few wind \ngusts could reach around 20 knots late this afternoon before \ndiminishing overnight as the front fully transitions out of the \nregion. Temperatures remain in the mid-to-upper 70s today & tomorrow \nas upper-level ridging briefly builds over the Appalachians, with \ndewpoints ranging generally in the 30s across most areas. As most of \nCONUS remains quiet off to our west, clear skies and light winds \nshould continue to dominate over the next 36-48 hours ahead of any \npotential remnants of Hurricane Isaias. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to arrive Saturday\nnight.\n\n\nLatest guidance show rain associated with remnants of Isaias \narriving in central VA Saturday evening, a little quicker than what \nit was shown yesterday, and spreading northeast across the rest of \nthe fcst area late Saturday night, reaching the Susquehanna River by \n12Z Sunday. The rain will be moderately intense during the overnight \nhours Sat night through Sunday morning, and begin to diminish in \nintensity mid afternoon Sunday as mid-level dry slot begins to move \nin from the southwest. Ensemble guidance shows generally one to two \ninches of rain falling, with the heaviest/most intense rainfall \nfalling in a roughly 12-hr period from 06Z-18Z Sun. Given the long \nduration and stratiform type, no flooding is expected. As for the \nrisk of severe wx, if it were to occur, it would be Sunday night, \nwith the greatest risk south of Point Lookout into the VA Tidewater \nregion. Some showers could linger into Monday morning as the mid-\nlevel circulation crosses the area. \n\nA strong cold front associated with a deepening northern stream \ntrough is then fcst to cross the area Tue night bringing autumn \nchill for the middle and second half of next week. \n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\n\nIn the immediate wake of the cold front, clear & dry conditions are \nexpected to persist over the region over the next 36-48 hours.\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated across all terminals in \nthe near term as high pressure builds overhead. KCHO could see a bit \nmore cloud cover between Friday and Saturday as tropical remnants \nfrom Hurricane Isaias begin to push north, but VFR conditions are \nstill expected to prevail through Saturday morning. \n\nBreezier winds from this afternoon will diminish overnight, flowing \nout of the W to WNW. Winds could begin to shift more northeasterly \non Saturday. \n\nRain is expected Saturday night through mid-afternoon Sunday, \nbecoming lighter and more showery Sunday evening. A thunderstorm \ncan't be ruled out Sunday night. Winds should strengthen Saturday \nnight into Sunday as pressure gradient tightens. Wouldn't rule out \nsome LLWS if a strong inversion develops. Showers should end by \nmidday Monday. Expect IFR conditions late Sunday night through \nSunday night with conditions improving Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds diminish below SCA criteria over the Chesapeake Bay later \ntonight. Light northwesterly winds shift easterly on Saturday, \nbecoming gustier by Sunday as tropical remnants from Hurricane \nIsaias begin to track east over the Mid-Atlantic. Additional SCAs \nremain a likelihood at this point until any remaining tropical \nremnants move through. \n\nSCA conditions are likely starting Saturday night and continuing \nthrough Sunday. Another period of SCA conditions is likely Tue night \ninto Wed with gales possible. \n\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\n\nMinor coastal flooding seems likely at sensitive locations Sunday \nevening due to rising water levels due to strengthening SE or S \nwinds combined with high astronomical tides due to a New Moon. \nUntil then, tide levels remain below action stage.\n\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT this evening for ANZ530-\n     531-535-538.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/SRT\nAVIATION...LFR/SRT\nMARINE...LFR/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791494297,"days":{"2026-10-08":{"hi":80,"lo":52,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":75,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":73,"lo":61,"pop":45,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":69,"lo":64,"pop":89,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":74,"lo":62,"pop":60,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-10-13":{"hi":78,"lo":58,"pop":11,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-14":{"hi":68,"lo":53,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791493200,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,24900,24900,24900,24900,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,24600,24300,23900,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.